  "slug": "environment-shape-regulatory-variation-1c31",
  "summary": "Field, long-read, ionomic, and genome datasets now resolve how genotype, environment, and haplotype structure shape gene regulation and stress traits in crops.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that measured plant phenotypes and transcripts depend on genetic background, environment, and the analytical frame used to interpret them. In switchgrass, field sampling across Michigan and Texas maps eQTL and GxE effects on gene regulation, selection, and leaf phosphorus traits. Pearl millet lines grown under drought in Senegal show that foliar ionomic stability is linked to flowering and biomass. Long-read transcriptomics of heat-stressed tetraploid potato resolves haplotype-specific expression, a heat-inducible allele, and a nucleus-targeted HSP40 isoform. Wheat nodal root anatomy differs by culm position beyond root area scaling, and allometric correction with GWAS reveals genetic associations distinct across root traits. A parent-of-origin-resolved Granny Smith genome shows that matched references expose sun-exposure and cold-storage responses that other references miss. Together, these studies point toward haplotype-aware, environment-explicit designs in crop genomics.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
